# [[ title .Name ]]

A [Quanarch](https://quanarch.org) project.

```
[[ .Name ]]/
├── go.work
├── packages/
│   └── [[ (index .Quas 0).Name ]]/
├── quanar/
└── quans/
    └── [[ (index .Quans 0).Name ]]/
```

- `packages/[[ (index .Quas 0).Name ]]`: the qua, the contract between the quanar and its quans
- `quanar`: the server; it loads the quans, runs each in its own process and does their work
- `quans/[[ (index .Quans 0).Name ]]`: a quan, business logic the quanar loads; it has no `main`

`go.work` holds the three together while you develop.

## Run

In two terminals:

```sh
# starts the quanar on port 3000
cd quanar && go run .

# builds the quan, serves it on port [[ (index .Quans 0).Port ]] and announces every new build
cd quans/[[ (index .Quans 0).Name ]] && go tool quan-build --watch
```

```sh
curl localhost:3000/[[ (index .Quans 0).Name ]]
curl -X POST localhost:3000/[[ (index .Quans 0).Name ]] -H 'Content-Type: application/json' -d '{"text":"hello"}'
curl localhost:3000/quans
```

The quan serves nothing itself. `api.Get("/[[ (index .Quans 0).Name ]]", …)` asks the quanar to listen
for it, and `db.Create(ctx, "[[ snake (index .Quans 0).Name ]]", …)` asks the quanar to store a row.
Stop the quan and the quanar stops listening at once; start it again and it is back.

On Linux each quan runs behind walls: Landlock, its own network and a memory ceiling. On other
systems it runs without them, and the quanar says so when it starts.
